// GENERATED by scripts/build-courses.ts from src/lib/courses/content — edit those files, then run: npm run courses
import type { LessonText } from '../types';
const text: LessonText[][] = [[{"say":"Calculus starts with limits: what value a function approaches as the input approaches a point.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"lim x→a f(x) = L means f(x) gets arbitrarily close to L as x approaches a (from both sides). The two one-sided limits must agree for the limit to exist."},{"t":"p","text":"Limit laws let you split sums, products and quotients. For polynomials, substitute directly. For 0/0 forms, factor and cancel, rationalize, or simplify. The squeeze theorem handles limits like lim x→0 sin x / x = 1."},{"t":"p","text":"The formal ε–δ definition: for every ε > 0 there is a δ > 0 so that 0 < |x − a| < δ implies |f(x) − L| < ε."},{"t":"ex","problem":"Find lim x→3 (x² − 9)/(x − 3).","steps":["Factor: (x − 3)(x + 3)/(x − 3) = x + 3","Substitute x = 3"],"answer":"6"}]},{"say":"Continuous functions have no breaks. Limits at infinity describe long-run behavior.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"f is continuous at a if f(a) is defined, lim x→a f(x) exists, and the two are equal. Discontinuities are removable (hole), jump, or infinite (vertical asymptote)."},{"t":"p","text":"The Intermediate Value Theorem: if f is continuous on [a, b] and N is between f(a) and f(b), then f(c) = N for some c in (a, b). It proves roots exist."},{"t":"p","text":"Limits at infinity: for rational functions compare degrees. Equal degrees → ratio of leading coefficients; bottom degree larger → 0; top degree larger → ±∞. These give horizontal asymptotes."},{"t":"ex","problem":"Find lim x→∞ (3x² + 1)/(5x² − x).","steps":["Equal degrees: ratio of leading coefficients"],"answer":"3/5"}]}],[{"say":"The derivative measures instantaneous rate of change: the slope of the tangent line.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Definition: f′(x) = lim h→0 [f(x + h) − f(x)]/h. Differentiable implies continuous (but not the reverse: |x| has a corner at 0)."},{"t":"p","text":"Rules: power rule d/dx xⁿ = nxⁿ⁻¹; constant multiple and sum rules; product rule (fg)′ = f′g + fg′; quotient rule (f/g)′ = (f′g − fg′)/g²."},{"t":"p","text":"Basic derivatives: (sin x)′ = cos x, (cos x)′ = −sin x, (eˣ)′ = eˣ, (ln x)′ = 1/x."},{"t":"ex","problem":"Differentiate f(x) = x³ sin x.","steps":["Product rule: (x³)′ sin x + x³ (sin x)′"],"answer":"3x² sin x + x³ cos x"}]},{"say":"Most real functions are compositions. The chain rule handles them.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Chain rule: d/dx f(g(x)) = f′(g(x)) · g′(x). Differentiate the outside, keep the inside, multiply by the derivative of the inside."},{"t":"p","text":"Implicit differentiation: differentiate both sides with respect to x, treating y as a function of x (so d/dx y² = 2y · dy/dx), then solve for dy/dx."},{"t":"p","text":"Related rates: write an equation linking quantities, differentiate with respect to time t, substitute known values, and solve for the unknown rate. Inverse trig and logarithmic differentiation extend the toolkit."},{"t":"ex","problem":"Differentiate y = (3x² + 1)⁵.","steps":["Outside: 5( )⁴; inside derivative: 6x"],"answer":"30x(3x² + 1)⁴"}]}],[{"say":"Derivatives tell us where functions rise, fall and peak, which lets us optimize.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Critical points occur where f′(x) = 0 or is undefined. f′ > 0 means increasing; f′ < 0 means decreasing. First derivative test: a sign change from + to − gives a local max."},{"t":"p","text":"Second derivative: f″ > 0 means concave up; f″ < 0 means concave down; inflection points occur where concavity changes. The extreme value theorem: a continuous function on a closed interval has an absolute max and min (check critical points and endpoints)."},{"t":"p","text":"Optimization: write the quantity to maximize or minimize as one variable, differentiate, set to zero, and confirm. The Mean Value Theorem and L'Hôpital's rule (for 0/0 or ∞/∞) are further tools."},{"t":"ex","problem":"Find two positive numbers summing to 20 with maximum product.","steps":["P = x(20 − x) = 20x − x²; P′ = 20 − 2x = 0"],"answer":"x = 10, so 10 and 10 (product 100)"}]},{"say":"Integration reverses differentiation and measures accumulated change, such as area under a curve.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Riemann sums approximate area with rectangles (left, right, midpoint); the definite integral is the limit as rectangles get thinner."},{"t":"p","text":"Antiderivatives: ∫xⁿ dx = xⁿ⁺¹/(n + 1) + C (n ≠ −1), ∫1/x dx = ln|x| + C, ∫eˣ dx = eˣ + C, ∫cos x dx = sin x + C."},{"t":"p","text":"Fundamental Theorem of Calculus: ∫ₐᵇ f(x) dx = F(b) − F(a) where F′ = f; and d/dx ∫ₐˣ f(t) dt = f(x). Substitution (u-sub) reverses the chain rule."},{"t":"ex","problem":"Evaluate ∫₀² 3x² dx.","steps":["Antiderivative: x³","2³ − 0³"],"answer":"8"}]}]];
export default text;
